EXT. LAKE - FOG - DAWN

A rowboat adrift on gray water. No shore in any direction. The oars lie shipped. A lantern burns without need of light.

KIRSTEN BLOMQVIST, forties, sits at the stern, wrapped in a blanket, eyes fixed on a fixed point in the mist. RAJESH IYER, fifties, her husband, sits at the bow with a thermos between his knees. They have not spoken in an hour.

He unscrews the cap. He pours half into the lid, passes it back along the boat without a word. She takes it. She drinks. She returns it.

He sets the lid on the thwart. He studies the water.

RAJESH
Did you hear something?

KIRSTEN
No.

RAJESH
There was a bell.

KIRSTEN
There's no bell.

He listens. The fog presses in. She pulls the blanket tighter and looks at his hands, which are shaking, and then at her own, which are not.

She reaches under the seat. She finds the second thermos. She sets it on the bench between them, unopened.

RAJESH
We'll want that later.

KIRSTEN
Yes.

He nods. She nods. They both look out at the gray.

Far off, faint, one clear note. A bell.

Neither of them turns.
